 QUALITY-03 reduces repeated CPU work without changing these boundaries. The bounded JSON parser performs its safety walk once before profile shape/reference checks; direct object validation still performs both. The walk inspects own property descriptors without invoking getters, including non-enumerable fields, and retains all limits. Trusted schema field lists are compiled when validators are created; no validation result is cached for mutable input. Short strings avoid character-array allocation while retaining Unicode code-point limits and ID control-character rejection. Account hydration groups ordered child rows by parent once instead of repeatedly scanning whole tables. Habit check commands reuse the pure check-in result for materialized-occurrence validation; an archived occurrence remains valid for explicit Undo through its original snapshot. See [performance measurement and constraints](docs/PERFORMANCE.md).
 
+The account record writer compares already-validated JSON values directly when deciding whether a row changed. Object key order is ignored; array order, field presence and scalar types remain significant, and numeric zero retains JSON equivalence. This private comparison is not an input validator. Canonical serialization remains unchanged for row identities, sync/import hashes and immutable replay fingerprints. No-op account commands still advance the account revision and receipt once, while unchanged rows and removal barriers retain their versions.
+
 `LocalStore.read` and `LocalStore.transaction` transfer owned records detached from retained storage, other calls and transaction callback objects. IndexedDB detaches its read results and clones the committed callback result; the memory adapter keeps equivalent isolation. `LocalRepository` can transfer the owned state into its snapshot without making a second whole-profile copy. Mutating a returned read, command result or replay must not change durable state, another caller's view or later results. The strict transaction and atomic receipt boundary remain unchanged.
 
 `parseStoredState` validates parsed JSON without normalizing it. `DeviceSession.dispatch` therefore reuses the command result's serialized text for no-op comparison instead of serializing the parsed result again. Validation still precedes optimistic publication, and only the transactional acknowledgment may report a save. No-op commands create no receipt or revision; unknown safe extensions and the original recovery source remain intact.
